"dolphin_shell.mst — the DD9 shell gate, rebuilt on UI.View (DolphinDart DD10).

 The DD9 shell proved Dolphin's `BorderLayout` could arrange real Win32
 windows — but through `WinView`, a hand-written adapter standing in for a
 view. This is the same gate with the stand-in removed: the container is a
 real `UI.ShellView`, the three arranged things are real `UI.View`s, and every
 method the layout reaches — `clientRectangle`, `rectangle:`, `layoutExtent:`,
 `subViewsDo:`, `adjustRectangle:`, `hasVisibleStyle` — is Dolphin's own.

 That is what retires `WinView`: `UI.View` already implements the whole
 protocol the adapter was written to supply. Nothing here re-supplies it.

 The only Smalltalk in this file is the APP — a shell that builds three
 sub-views and lays them out north/centre/south. That is the code an
 application author writes, and it is the only kind of Smalltalk that should
 be hand-written from here on."
ShellView subclass: DolphinShell [
    | northView centreView southView relayouts |

    DolphinShell class >> edgeHeight [ ^28 ]

    relayouts [ ^relayouts ifNil: [ 0 ] ]
    northView [ ^northView ]
    centreView [ ^centreView ]
    southView [ ^southView ]

    "Build the sub-views. Each is a real `UI.View` created by Dolphin's own
     `create`, parented to this shell — `parentView:` is what makes it a child
     window, and `addSubView:` is what puts it in the layout's reach."
    buildViews [
        relayouts := 0.
        northView := self makeChild: DolphinShell edgeHeight.
        southView := self makeChild: DolphinShell edgeHeight.
        "The centre declares no preferred height: it takes what is left."
        centreView := self makeChild: nil.
        self layoutManager: (BorderLayout new
            north: northView;
            south: southView;
            center: centreView;
            yourself).
        ^self
    ]

    "Dolphin's own idiom, which is narrower than it looks: build the view
     UNREALIZED, set its parent, and let `addSubView:` realize it.

     `View>>basicAddSubView:` is `aView parentView: self. aView show.`, and its
     own comment on that line says it forces creation — adding a sub-view is
     what creates its window. An earlier version here called `create` and then
     `addSubView:`, which asked for the window twice; `parentView:` is
     documented as usable only before realization, for the same reason."
    makeChild: aHeightOrNil [
        | v |
        v := View new.
        v parentView: self.
        "Two sends, not one. `preferredExtent:` records the wanted size;
         `usePreferredExtent:` is a separate flag (bit 256 of `flags`) that
         decides whether the layout consults it at all —

             layoutExtent: ctx
                 ^self usePreferredExtent
                     ifTrue: [self actualPreferredExtent: ctx]
                     ifFalse: [ctx extentOf: self]

         With the extent set but the flag clear, BorderLayout asked the view
         for its CURRENT extent and docked the edges at the 100x100 creation
         default — a plausible-looking arrangement that simply ignored what
         was asked for."
        aHeightOrNil isNil ifFalse: [
            v preferredExtent: 0 @ aHeightOrNil.
            v usePreferredExtent: true ].
        self addSubView: v.
        ^v
    ]

    "How many children Dolphin's own sibling walk finds.

     Counted through `subViewsDo:` DELIBERATELY, rather than from the three
     ivars this class already holds. That walk is the method that spun forever
     before the floor's `#h` handle-return convention — it asks Windows for
     the first child and follows `GW_HWNDNEXT` until the chain ends in NULL —
     so counting through it is what proves the termination, and an ivar count
     would prove nothing at all."
    subViewCount [
        | n |
        n := 0.
        self subViewsDo: [ :each | n := n + 1 ].
        ^n
    ]

    "The raw sibling chain, as handles, for diagnosis. `subViewCount` counting
     more children than were built has two very different causes — extra
     windows, or the same windows revisited — and only the handles tell them
     apart."
    childHandleReport [
        | out child n |
        out := WriteStream on: String new.
        child := User32 getWindow: handle uCmd: 5.
        n := 0.
        [ child isNil or: [ n > 20 ] ] whileFalse: [
            out nextPutAll: child printString;
                nextPutAll: '(parent='; nextPutAll: (User32 getParent: child) printString;
                nextPutAll: ') '.
            n := n + 1.
            child := User32 getWindow: child uCmd: 2 ].
        ^out contents
    ]

    "── FOCUS, carried over from the retired `shell_probe` ────────────────
     `UI.View` has `setFocus`/`hasFocus` already; nothing here re-supplies
     view protocol. Kept as probe methods so the gate names an intent rather
     than sending SetFocus itself."
    focusNorth [ ^northView setFocus ]
    focusSouth [ ^southView setFocus ]

    "One layout pass, driven the way Dolphin drives it."
    relayout [
        relayouts := self relayouts + 1.
        self layout.
        ^relayouts
    ]

    "Resize, then relayout — the gate calls this to prove the arrangement
     tracks the container's client area."
    resizeTo: w by: h [
        User32
            setWindowPos: handle
            hWndInsertAfter: 0
            x: 0 y: 0 cx: w cy: h
            uFlags: 16r16.        "SWP_NOMOVE | SWP_NOZORDER | SWP_NOACTIVATE"
        ^self relayout
    ]

    "The client extent BorderLayout divides up, read from Dolphin's own
     `clientRectangle` rather than from a Win32 call in the gate."
    clientWidth [ ^self clientRectangle width ]
    clientHeight [ ^self clientRectangle height ]

    "Each sub-view's rectangle, from Dolphin's own `getRect` — the same
     accessor `LayoutPlacement` compares against when deciding whether a
     placement is a real change."
    rectOf: aView [
        | r |
        r := aView getRect.
        ^Array with: r origin x with: r origin y with: r width with: r height
    ]

    "How many CHILD WINDOWS each step of `makeChild:` leaves behind.

     `makeChild:` is meant to produce exactly one window per call. When the
     sibling walk finds three times as many as were built, the question is
     which step made the extras — so this runs the same steps one at a time
     and reports the raw child count after each."
    diagChildSteps [
        | out v |
        out := WriteStream on: String new.
        out nextPutAll: 'start='; nextPutAll: self rawChildCount printString.
        v := View new.
        out nextPutAll: ' new='; nextPutAll: self rawChildCount printString.
        v parentView: self.
        out nextPutAll: ' parentView='; nextPutAll: self rawChildCount printString.
        v create.
        out nextPutAll: ' create='; nextPutAll: self rawChildCount printString.
        self addSubView: v.
        out nextPutAll: ' addSubView='; nextPutAll: self rawChildCount printString.
        v show.
        out nextPutAll: ' show='; nextPutAll: self rawChildCount printString.
        ^out contents
    ]

    "Children counted straight off Windows, with no `lookupWindow:` filter —
     so it sees windows the registry does not know about too."
    rawChildCount [
        | child n |
        child := User32 getWindow: handle uCmd: 5.
        n := 0.
        [ child isNil or: [ n > 40 ] ] whileFalse: [
            n := n + 1.
            child := User32 getWindow: child uCmd: 2 ].
        ^n
    ]

    "A step-by-step build, answering WHERE it stopped. A gate that only sees
     the final nil has to infer the step; this names it."
    diagStep [
        | v |
        flags isNil ifTrue: [ ^'shell flags nil' ].
        v := View new.
        v parentView: self.
        v create.
        self addSubView: v.
        ^'ok'
    ]
]

